    {
      "id": "c9-l2",
      "title": "Factorials",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "5! (5 factorial) is 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120. Notice: 5! is just 5 × 4!. And 4! is 4 × 3!, and so on, down to 1! = 1.",
        "That's a perfect fit for recursion: factorial(n) is n * factorial(n - 1), with the base case factorial(1) = 1.",
        "The computer keeps every waiting call on the stack: factorial(5) waits for factorial(4), which waits for factorial(3) … then they all finish in reverse order."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int factorial(int n) {",
          "    if (n <= 1)",
          "        return 1;",
          "    return n * factorial(n - 1);",
          "}",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int i;",
          "    for (i = 1; i <= 6; i++)",
          "        printf(\"%d! = %d\\n\", i, factorial(i));",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "1! = 1",
          "2! = 2",
          "3! = 6",
          "4! = 24",
          "5! = 120",
          "6! = 720"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c9-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is 4!?",
          "options": [
            "24",
            "10",
            "16",
            "4"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 24."
        },
        {
          "id": "c9-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is the base case of factorial?",
          "options": [
            "n <= 1 returns 1",
            "n == 10",
            "n * n",
            "There is none"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "1! (and 0!) are 1."
        },
        {
